## 3. Business case and success metrics

### The cost being addressed

Manual first-line triage in a four-team application-maintenance organisation is
a per-ticket cost paid by a senior engineer — reading, judging severity, judging
ownership, and moving the ticket. Three failure modes compound it:

1. **Latency to the right team.** A misrouted ticket does not start its real clock
   until it is re-routed; that time is invisible in most SLA reporting because the
   SLA clock started at creation.
2. **Inconsistency.** The same ticket triaged by two people, or by one person on
   Monday and Friday, gets different priorities. That variance is what makes SLA
   commitments unreliable rather than the SLA targets themselves.
3. **No reconstructable rationale.** Nobody can explain months later why a ticket
   was a P1, which makes both audit and process improvement impossible.

### Metrics, and how each is actually measured

| Metric | Definition | Measurement source | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Classification accuracy** | Predicted category == gold category | `score_triage_accuracy()` over `tickets.true_category` where `held_out=true` | Measured |
| **Routing precision** | Predicted team == gold team, per team and overall | `true_team`, with a per-team precision/recall table and a confusion matrix | Measured |
| **Priority MAE** | Mean absolute error in priority band distance | `true_severity` vs computed | Measured |
| **Override rate** | Human overrides ÷ decisions | `tickets.overridden_by` count ÷ total, on Control Tower | Measured |
| **Groundedness** | Share of answer claims supported by retrieved context | LLM-scored, thresholded at 0.25 / 0.5 | Measured |
| **Hallucination rate** | `1 − groundedness` on the KB eval set | `eval_results` | Measured |
| **Refusal correctness** | The eval set includes questions that **must** be refused | `EVAL_SET` (12 questions, ≥2 unanswerable) | Measured |
| **Fairness** | Per-team over/under-severitisation rates | Fairness table on `/evals` | Measured |
| **Cost per triage** | Tokens × tier price, per ticket | `triage_runs.cost_usd`, `Trace` | Measured |
| **P95 latency** | Per request and per stage | `usage_summary()` | Measured |
| **Time-to-first-touch** | Ticket creation → routed status | Derivable from `tickets.created_at` / `updated_at` | Leading indicator |
| **Mean time to resolution** | Requires resolved-state data over weeks | — | **Not measurable in a hackathon window**; the honest position is that TTFT and routing precision are the leading indicators that drive it |

> **Stated plainly:** MTTR reduction cannot be demonstrated in 24 hours with
> synthetic data. Claiming a percentage would be fabrication. What is demonstrable
> is that every ticket is triaged in seconds instead of minutes, routed correctly
> at a measured precision, and that every misroute is caught by a human whose
> correction is captured as a training signal.

