## 8. Agentic architecture

### 8.1 Design pattern

**Supervised graph with corrective loops and a human gate.** Not an autonomous
agent loop: control flow is explicit, node order is fixed, both backward edges are
capped at one iteration, and the terminal action is gated on a human decision or a
narrow auto-approve band.

### 8.2 Agent roles

| Node | Role | Model tier | Contract |
|---|---|---|---|
| `normalize` | Ingestion & sanitation | fast | raw ticket → masked `Ticket` + guardrail verdict |
| `enrich` | Evidence gathering | retrieval + fast | ticket → 8 chunks + duplicate verdict |
| `grade` | Evidence quality control (CRAG) | fast | chunks → filtered chunks + keep/rewrite/none |
| `classify` | Categorisation | standard | → `TriageVerdict` (category, subcategory, service, confidence, rationale) |
| `assess` | Severity & priority | **deep** | → `SeverityVerdict` (severity, priority_score, confidence, rationale) |
| `route` | Ownership | standard | → `RoutingVerdict` (team, confidence, rationale) + suggested first action |
| `reflect` | Self-critique | **deep** | → `ReflectionVerdict`; may only lower confidence |
| `verify` | Output guardrails | fast | → groundedness, blocked, guardrails fired |
| `gate` | Human-in-the-loop | deterministic | → `needs_human`, `escalation_reason` |
| `sync` | Assembly & persistence | — | → `TriageDecision`, status, `triage_runs` row |

### 8.3 Collaboration model and handoffs

State is a `TypedDict` whose meaningful values are Pydantic models. Each node
returns a partial state update; LangGraph merges it. **No node passes prose to
another node.** A model response that fails validation becomes the schema default,
which is a safe, known value rather than corrupted state — for example a
`RoutingVerdict` defaulting to deterministic keyword routing with confidence 0.

### 8.4 Retry, reflection and escalation

| Mechanism | Bound | Trigger | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|---|
| CRAG rewrite | 1 | grader returns `rewrite` | Re-enter `enrich` with a rewritten query |
| Reflection retry | 1 | `reflect` reports missing evidence | Re-enter `enrich` |
| Input blocked | — | guardrail fires at `normalize` | Short-circuit to `gate`; downstream nodes never see the text |
| Duplicate detected | conf ≥ 0.75 | `enrich` duplicate check | `duplicate_of` set; `gate` escalates |
| Node exception | — | any uncaught error | `needs_human=True`, `status="failed"`, row preserved |

> **Implementation note worth stating.** Retry counters are incremented **inside
> the nodes**, never in the conditional-edge routers. LangGraph discards state
> mutations made inside a router — an earlier version incremented there, so the
> counter never persisted and a model that kept requesting a rewrite looped
> `enrich` indefinitely, hanging syncs. Routers now only read state; nodes own
> their own budget. This class of bug only appears under real model behaviour, not
> against a canned test response.

**Escalation ladder on failure:**

```
deep model → fast model → deterministic catalogue routing → unassigned human queue
```

The system degrades to "a human sees it". It never degrades to silence, and never
to a guess presented as certainty.

### 8.5 Autonomy boundaries

| Decision | Autonomy |
|---|---|
| Classify, assess, route, draft a rationale | Full — read-only, reversible |
| Write priority/label/comment to Jira | **Only** if `confidence ≥ 0.50` **and** priority ∈ {High, Medium, Low} **and** no guardrail fired **and** not a duplicate |
| Any `Highest` priority decision | **Never autonomous** — always human approval |
| Any decision with `confidence < 0.50`, `evidence_coverage < 0.50` or `band_margin < 0.10` | **Never autonomous** |
| Assign to a named individual | **Not supported by design** |
| Execute a remediation action | **Not supported by design** |

### 8.6 The KB chat graph

A second, simpler graph — `plan → retrieve → generate → verify` — powers
`/api/chat` (multi-session assistant) and `/api/chatbot` (a single server-pinned
session per user, reachable from every page). `plan` decides whether retrieval is
needed at all; if `verify` finds the answer ungrounded, the graph routes back to
retrieval **once** with a decomposed query.

**Memory** is retrieval infrastructure, not chat polish: short-term memory replays
the last 12 turns; long-term memory is a rolling LLM summary refreshed every 6
turns, which is what lets a follow-up like *"what about the second one?"* be
rewritten into a standalone retrieval query.

Three question types bypass RAG deliberately: greetings, memory-recall questions,
and **aggregate ticket questions**, which are answered by SQL tools and only
narrated by the model.

