## 12. Responsible AI and guardrails

### 12.1 Privacy

**Two-pass de-identification at ingest.** Deterministic regex catches structured
identifiers (email, phone, SSN, AWS keys, Azure connection strings, JWTs, PEM
blocks) and domain PII (employee ID, internal IP). An optional LLM pass catches
contextual identifiers (names, organisations) for documents over 120 characters.

Secrets are **irreversibly** redacted (`[REDACTED_AWS_KEY]`); structured
identifiers become **reversible** tokens (`[EMAIL_1]`) so a masked ticket remains
readable and internally consistent.

**Ticket IDs and error codes are deliberately excluded from every pattern.** They
are the exact strings BM25 depends on; masking them would blind retrieval to the
one thing that reliably identifies a recurring failure.

**Output is scanned again** for leaks before anything is returned or written.

### 12.2 Prompt injection

Defence in depth, cheapest first:

1. Length limit (4000 characters)
2. Six high-precision regex signatures — microseconds, cannot hallucinate
3. PII detection and masking
4. **Only if still ambiguous**, an LLM injection classifier; blocks at
   confidence ≥ 0.70

Plus the structural defence: untrusted text is fenced as data in every prompt, and
a blocked ticket short-circuits to `gate` so the injected text never reaches the
classifier or the assessor.

### 12.3 Hallucination control

| Control | Threshold |
|---|---|
| Groundedness score on generated answers | refuse below **0.25**; ship with a visible caveat between 0.25 and 0.5 |
| Policy violation | blocks regardless of groundedness score |
| Banned phrasings | first-person claims that the current ticket is resolved, that the assistant resolved something, or an invented ETA not sourced from the SLA policy |
| Aggregate figures | answered by SQL; the model only narrates — groundedness exact by construction |
| Citation requirement | rationale must cite `[C#]` for any retrieved evidence used |

Banned-phrasing rules are anchored to first-person claims about the *current*
ticket, so citing a genuinely resolved precedent does not false-positive.

### 12.4 Bias and fairness

The `/evals` fairness table reports **per-team routing precision and recall** and
**per-team over/under-severitisation rates**. The failure mode being watched for is
systematic misrouting toward one team (which quietly becomes that team's on-call
burden) or systematic severity inflation for one platform.

**Known limitation:** fairness is measured on synthetic labelled data. Real
production distributions will differ, and the eval set must be regenerated from
real labelled history before any production claim.

### 12.5 Explainability

Every decision carries: the retrieved passages it was based on, an inline-cited
rationale in plain English aimed at an on-call engineer, the confidence gates and
**which gate limited the score**, the escalation reason when a human is required,
and a persisted `triage_runs` row with the full decision JSON, model, tier, tokens,
cost, latency and trace ID.

### 12.6 Unauthorised tool execution

`ai/tools.py::call()` is the single choke point. It enforces role scope before
anything runs and audits denials as `tool.denied`. `ticket_update` — the only
write-capable tool — additionally verifies the decision's status or auto-approve
band **itself**, so a route that sets a status is never sufficient on its own.
`POST /api/tickets/<id>/approve` refuses with 409 if the triage never completed,
closing a real gap where an incomplete decision could write a blank comment onto a
live Jira issue.

