## 14. Integration architecture

### 14.1 The `TicketSource` interface

```python
class TicketSource:
    name: str
    def fetch_since(self, watermark: str | None, limit: int = 50) -> list[dict]: ...
    def update(self, external_id: str, fields: dict) -> None: ...
    def add_comment(self, external_id: str, text: str) -> None: ...
    def transition(self, external_id: str, status: str) -> None: ...
```

Four methods. Everything above this line — the graph, guardrails, database, UI —
is vendor-agnostic. Two implementations ship: `JiraSource` and `SyntheticSource`.

### 14.2 Jira Cloud adapter

| Operation | Call |
|---|---|
| Fetch | `POST /rest/api/3/search/jql` — `project = {KEY} AND updated >= "{watermark}" ORDER BY updated ASC`, `maxResults=50` |
| Update | `PUT /rest/api/3/issue/{key}` |
| Comment | `POST /rest/api/3/issue/{key}/comment` (ADF body) |
| Transition | `GET` then `POST /rest/api/3/issue/{key}/transitions` |

**Auth:** HTTP Basic — base64(`JIRA_EMAIL:JIRA_API_TOKEN`).
**Resilience:** 3 retries, exponential backoff from 1.5s, honours `Retry-After` on
429, treats 5xx as retryable. A failed cycle returns zero tickets and logs; it
never crashes the poller.

**Inbound field mapping** (`issue_to_ticket_dict` — one function, used by both the
poller and the webhook, so the two paths cannot drift):

| Jira | Internal | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `key` | `external_id` | Dedupe key with `source` |
| `summary` | `title` | |
| `description` (ADF) | `body` | Flattened to text; tables and code blocks lose formatting but keep content |
| `components[0]` | `application` | |
| `reporter` / `assignee` | `reporter` / `assignee` | `displayName` — Jira Cloud withholds email by default under GDPR |
| `priority.name` | `raw["priority"]` | **The reporter's own claim**, preserved for reported-vs-computed comparison |
| `created` | `created_at` | The real incident start; the SLA clock uses this, not poll time. Used only on row creation, so a later edit never resets the SLA |
| `updated` | `updated_at` | Drives the watermark |

**Outbound write-back:**

| What | How |
|---|---|
| Priority | `fields.priority.name` — `Highest`/`High`/`Medium`/`Low`. **By name, not ID** — Jira priority IDs are per-site and not guaranteed stable |
| Team | Label `AWS`/`AZR`/`GCP`/`OPS`, with the other three removed in the same call so exactly one is ever present |
| Rationale | ADF comment: decision, confidence, cited reasoning, recommended first action |
| Workflow | Transition to `In Progress` (triaged/routed/synced) or `Done` (resolved). A transition name absent from that board's workflow is skipped with a warning |
| Custom fields | Written **only** if `JIRA_FIELD_*` is configured; empty default means "do not write" rather than writing to the wrong place |
| Assignee | **Never written** — routing is to a team by policy |

**Idempotency:** dedupe on `(source, external_id)`; watermark persisted in
`sync_state`; `sync_attempts` and `last_error` on every write; permanent failure →
`status="failed"` dead letter; a manager's override is never clobbered by re-sync.

### 14.3 Extending to ServiceNow

A `ServiceNowSource` implementing the same four methods against the Table API
(`/api/now/table/incident`) with `sys_updated_on` as the watermark. The mapping
work is: `short_description → title`, `description → body`, `cmdb_ci →
application`, `urgency`/`impact` → the reporter's priority claim, and
`work_notes` for the rationale write-back. **No change is required to the graph,
the guardrails, the database, or the UI.** Both sources can run concurrently
because tickets key on `(source, external_id)`.

### 14.4 Notification integration

SLA warnings are sent via **Resend's HTTPS API** rather than SMTP, chosen because
ports 25/465/587 are commonly blocked outbound on corporate networks that allow
443 — confirmed on the deployment host. One transport, one thing to configure, no
SMTP fallback. Recipients are looked up from `users.email` where the role is
`admin` or `manager`, so adding a manager account is the only action needed to
change who is notified. `sla_warning_sent_at` makes it one alert per ticket, not a
repeat every 15-minute cycle.

