## 17. Testing and quality assurance

### 17.1 Test data design

The 500-ticket synthetic corpus is generated with deliberate defects, because a
triage system tested only on clean input is untested:

| Property | Why |
|---|---|
| Typos and half-pasted stack traces | Real reporters write badly under pressure |
| Duplicate reports of one outage | Exercises the duplicate detector |
| **Planted secrets** (keys, connection strings, JWTs) | Exercises the redaction path — the test is that they never reach the index |
| Prompt-injection attempts in ticket bodies | Exercises the input guard end to end |
| Varied severity distribution | Prevents an eval score that is really just class imbalance |
| Tickets with missing evidence | Exercises `evidence_coverage` and the human gate |
| **100 held-out tickets, never embedded** | The exam the system cannot study for |

### 17.2 Coverage beyond accuracy

| Dimension | How it is checked |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | Held-out labelled set with a confusion matrix |
| Groundedness | LLM-scored per answer, thresholded, persisted |
| Hallucination | `1 − groundedness` plus banned-phrasing scan |
| Refusal | Eval set includes unanswerable questions that must be refused |
| Latency and cost | Per-stage traces, p95 in `usage_summary()` |
| Reliability | Dead-letter counts, `sync_attempts`, `last_error` |
| Traceability | Every decision reconstructible from `triage_runs` + `audit_log` |
| Safety | Injection corpus, PII leak scan, tool-denial audit |
| Consistency | `db/inspect_db.py` cross-store `DRIFT` check |

### 17.3 Gaps

No `pytest` suite, no CI pipeline, and no load test. Verification is via the eval
endpoints, the cross-store inspector and manual scenario runs. A production
hardening pass would add unit tests for the pure functions first — `_band_margin`,
`_evidence_coverage`, `_score_confidence`, `normalize_priority`,
`sla_elapsed_fraction` — since those are deterministic and carry the decision
logic.

