## 18. Non-functional characteristics

### 18.1 Performance

| Operation | Observed shape |
|---|---|
| One triage | 4–6 sequential model calls; deep tier dominates |
| Sync batch | 50 tickets max per cycle, 4 in parallel |
| Per-ticket timeout | `LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS × 6` (360s), then dead-lettered |
| Retrieval | Chroma HNSW + BM25 over the in-memory index; sub-second at this corpus size |
| Queue read | Pure SQL, no vector search — listing tickets never touches Chroma |

**Latency budget by design:** the guardrail order is deterministic-first so the
median ticket pays microseconds, not a model call. Retrieval fan-out, batch
embedding and the parallel groundedness/policy check all use a shared bounded
thread pool.

> **A concurrency bug worth documenting.** Ticket-level parallelism uses a
> **dedicated** `ThreadPoolExecutor`, not the shared fan-out pool. Submitting
> tickets to the shared pool nested fan-out inside fan-out: each ticket occupied a
> worker and then blocked waiting for a worker from the same fully-busy pool — a
> real deadlock, hit live during the build. Two independent pools have no shared
> resource cycle.

### 18.2 Scalability

| Dimension | Today | Path to production |
|---|---|---|
| Ticket volume | 50 per poll cycle, 4 concurrent | Replace the thread poller with a queue consumer (SQS/Service Bus); the `ingest_and_triage()` entry point is already a single idempotent unit of work |
| Corpus size | Chroma with HNSW, single node | pgvector or a managed vector service; one module changes |
| Concurrent users | Flask dev server, single process | Gunicorn/uvicorn workers behind a load balancer; move rate-limit state to Redis |
| Relational volume | SQLite, single file | Postgres — SQLAlchemy models are portable; the additive migration helper becomes Alembic |
| Model throughput | Gateway rate limits | Per-tier concurrency caps and a request queue; the tier split already reduces deep-tier pressure |
| Cost | Per-run `cost_usd` recorded | The tier split is the primary lever; response caching for repeated KB questions is the next |

### 18.3 Resilience

Every dependency has a defined degraded mode:

| Dependency fails | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| Model gateway | Local Ollama for all chat tiers |
| Embedding service | Local `gte-large` with a loud warning and a reseed requirement |
| Jira | Retry with backoff; then zero tickets this cycle; poller survives |
| Any model call | Schema default; `route` falls back to deterministic keyword routing |
| Retrieval quality | CRAG rewrite, then "no precedent" — never a silent bad match |
| The graph itself | `needs_human=True`, `status="failed"`, row preserved for a human |
| Process restart | Watermark resumed from `sync_state` |
| Email transport | Warning logged; SLA state unchanged so the next cycle retries |

**No single point of failure degrades to silence.** Every path terminates in a
human seeing the ticket.

